From 264cbd0555201189bf1c4d3736f3997a46bbd2ac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: omp <omp@local>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 00:10:17 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 21/23] tests: cover the series with Wine's own test suite

The six patches were verified against Power BI Desktop and against the probes in tools/winapi/, but nothing in
Wine's test suite fails when one of them is reverted, so a regression can only be found by running the
application again.  This adds the regression tests, in the test suite of the module each patch touches, each
written to judge the Windows behaviour the patch restored.  Reverting any one patch makes its test fail; see
docs/WINE_TESTS.md in the working checkout for the run and for that negative control, patch by patch.

  * dlls/ntdll/tests/impersonate.c (0002, new file) - NtImpersonateAnonymousToken on the calling thread
    succeeds and leaves it impersonating the anonymous logon: user S-1-5-7, primary group S-1-1-0, one group
    S-1-1-0, TokenImpersonation at SecurityImpersonation level, and RevertToSelf() takes the thread token away
    again.  A null and an invalid thread handle are STATUS_INVALID_HANDLE.  The test also calls the kernelbase
    wrapper (ImpersonateAnonymousToken), which is how an application reaches the call.
  * dlls/secur32/tests/secur32.c (0005) - GetUserNameExW's NameUserPrincipal and NameDnsDomain answer
    "user@dns.domain" and "dns.domain\user" when the machine is in a domain, with the two-call protocol the
    application uses ((NULL buffer) sizing call -> ERROR_MORE_DATA with the required size, then the call with
    that size -> the name) and ERROR_MORE_DATA with an untouched buffer for a buffer that is too small.  The
    machine's DNS domain is the registry value Wine reads it from, which the test sets when the machine is in
    no domain and restores afterwards, so the answering branch is covered on a host that is in no domain;
    without a domain both formats keep answering ERROR_NONE_MAPPED, which is what Windows does for an account
    outside a domain.  The same file also holds patch 0004's contract where a running test can reach it: the
    buffer LsaCallAuthenticationPackage returns is freed with LsaFreeReturnBuffer, and the status has to be
    success - it was VirtualFree()'s BOOL, so a *successful* free reported SEC_E_INVALID_HANDLE.
  * dlls/secur32/tests/negotiate.c (0003 and 0004) - the Negotiate loopback exchange is SPNEGO: three client
    rounds and two server rounds, the client's first token a NegTokenInit (0x60) and its second a NegTokenResp
    (0xa1) reported with SEC_I_CONTINUE_NEEDED - the round shape a caller that transmits only on
    SEC_I_CONTINUE_NEEDED needs, which is the failure this patch is about - a last round with an empty token
    and SEC_E_OK, and a NegTokenResp as the server's first answer.  The existing loop is extended to drive
    both roles to completion, which the extra round requires; the exchange uses the W entry points, the ones
    the provider calls; and the helpers keep the output buffer the test owns, because a call that has no token
    to send hands back no buffer for it (the test no longer assumes the descriptor still holds its own
    pointer).  The post-handshake context queries are made on the W side, which is where the package info is
    the SecPkgInfoW the LSA package allocated in this process - the buffer FreeContextBuffer hands to
    LsaFreeReturnBuffer (0004), whose status is asserted.  A second assertion for 0004 is added to
    test_Negotiate, on the response buffer of LsaCallAuthenticationPackage.
  * dlls/oledb32/tests/convert.c (0006) - IDataConvert::DataConvert converts a VARIANT source to DBTYPE_I8
    (VT_I4 and VT_I8) and to DBTYPE_BOOL (VT_BOOL, and VT_I4 0 and 1), with the destination length and status
    the conversion library reports for every other VARIANT destination; CanConvert already advertised both,
    which is what made the failure a failed row read rather than a rejected binding.
  * dlls/wintypes/tests/wintypes.c (0001) - RoResolveNamespace answers the metadata file a namespace is named
    after and nothing for "Windows", reports the sub-namespaces of "Windows", and rejects an empty name with
    E_INVALIDARG.  RoGetMetaDataFile resolves a type to the file that describes it and answers
    RO_E_METADATA_NAME_IS_NAMESPACE, RO_E_METADATA_NAME_NOT_FOUND and RO_E_METADATA_INVALID_TYPE_FORMAT for a
    name that is a namespace, an unknown type, and a name with an empty part around its last dot; the reader
    case is a todo_wine, because it needs IMetaDataImport2 and Wine's rometadata.dll implements only
    IMetaDataImport.  The ApiInformation presence queries the patch implements make assertions in this file
    that were todo_wine stop being todo (IsTypePresent and IsEnumNamedValuePresent now answer S_OK), and one
    that was not is marked todo_wine with its reason: a NULL member name is answered FALSE rather than refused.
    The two existing RoResolveNamespace cases lose their todo_wine and skip when the prefix has no Windows
    metadata files instead of passing by expecting the call to fail.

The expectations are Windows', from the probes named in the individual patches, apart from the three the
patches themselves record as not reproducible on the host used for them (the domain-configured GetUserNameExW
answer, judged by shape and by the sizing protocol; the RoGetMetaDataFile reader; and the NULL member name
above, which is marked todo_wine rather than asserted against the patch).

scripts/run_wine_tests.sh runs the five programs against the fork's build - it creates the test prefix, stages
the .winmd files the resolution tests read and reports RESULT: PASS or FAIL.

Merged with the AutoCAD series' tests patch (acad 0017): dlls/secur32/tests/secur32.c is the union of both.
The two patches add the identical testGetUserNameEx_domain() to that file, so it exists
once; this commit additionally carries the LsaFreeReturnBuffer() assertion that only the
Power BI series has.  Every other file of this patch is disjoint from acad 0017.
